---
// src/pages/what-is-yini-and-why.astro
import GitHubSocialProof from '../../components/GitHubSocialProof.astro'
import CONFIG from '../../config/conf'
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

/*
    NOTE: This page is intended for very first time users:
    - That has Zero context of what YINI is.
    - Even more focused than the front page (where users probably has been forwarden from somewhere else).

    1. What YINI is
    2. Why it exists
    3. Why they should care
    4. How to try it in under 60 seconds
*/
---

<BaseLayout
    headTitle="What is YINI and Why?"
    headDescription="YINI is a clear, human-friendly, text-based configuration format — less verbose than XML and JSON, simpler than YAML, and more expressive than INI."
>
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-2 text-center">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-5xl">
            What is YINI and Why?
        </h1>

        <!-- <GitHubSocialProof /> -->

        <p class="my-6 text-base md:text-lg">
            {/* HERO: Elevator pitch */}
            YINI is a clear, human-friendly, text-based configuration format — less
            verbose than JSON, simpler than YAML, and more expressive than INI.
        </p>
        {/* Positioning line */}
        <p class="my-6">
            Designed to stay readable as files grow, with explicit structure and
            predictable parsing.
        </p>

        <div
            class="my-4 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3 pt-10"
        >
            <a
                href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.getStarted.url}
                class="btn btn-primary"
            >
                Get Started
            </a>

            <a
                href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.playground.url}
                class="inline-block underline"
            >
                Playground
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="my-6">
        {/* The "big idea" */}
        <h2>✨ The simple idea</h2>
        <p>
            YINI combines the familiar simplicity of INI with structured values
            similar to JSON, explicit section markers for nesting, and parser
            modes for flexible editing or stricter validation.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-2">
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>INI</strong> → simple <code>key = value</code>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>JSON</strong> → useful types such as arrays, objects, booleans,
                and numbers
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Headings</strong> → clear section markers such as <code
                    >^</code
                >, <code>^^</code>, and <code>^^^</code>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Parser modes</strong> → lenient for everyday editing, strict
                when stronger validation is needed
            </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="mt-2">
            That's YINI — familiar, readable, structured, and practical to
            validate.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="my-6 pb-6">
        <h2>🧯 Why does YINI exist?</h2>
        Many configuration formats are useful, but each comes with trade-offs when
        files are written and maintained by humans:
        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>INI</strong> is simple, but often too flat.
                <blockquote style="margin-top: .5rem; margin-bottom: .25rem">
                    <strong
                        >YINI keeps <code>key = value</code>, but adds nested
                        sections.</strong
                    >
                </blockquote>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>JSON</strong> is explicit, but punctuation-heavy to edit
                by hand.
                <blockquote style="margin-top: .5rem; margin-bottom: .25rem">
                    YINI keeps structured values with less required punctuation.
                </blockquote>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>YAML</strong> is flexible, but indentation can affect meaning.
                <blockquote style="margin-top: .5rem; margin-bottom: .25rem">
                    YINI uses section markers instead of whitespace to define
                    structure.
                </blockquote>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>XML</strong> is structured, but verbose for configuration.
                <blockquote style="margin-top: .5rem; margin-bottom: .25rem">
                    YINI supports hierarchy without opening and closing tags.
                </blockquote>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>TOML</strong> is configuration-oriented, but deeply nested
                tables can become harder to scan.
                <blockquote style="margin-top: .5rem; margin-bottom: .25rem">
                    YINI makes nesting visible through repeated section markers.
                </blockquote>
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            YINI is designed as a practical middle ground: familiar
            configuration syntax, explicit nested sections, useful data types,
            indentation-insensitive structure, and predictable parsing rules.
        </p>
        <p>
            The goal is not to replace every configuration format. YINI is
            intentionally JSON-friendly: JSON remains a strong choice for APIs,
            generated output, data exchange, and many existing tools. YINI
            focuses on human-authored configuration, and the CLI can convert
            YINI files to JSON when JSON is the better format for the next step.
        </p>
        <p>
            It is especially suitable for local or internal settings/config
            files where you want a clear, predictable file that people can
            understand and hand-edit safely.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-2 pb-6">
        <!-- <h2>📝 Example</h2> -->
        <h3>Example of YINI code:</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="/samples/basic.yini.png"
                ><img
                    class="mt-0 mb-1 rounded-lg border-2 border-black/50"
                    src="/samples/basic.yini.png"
                    alt="File basic.yini"
                /></a
            >
            Source file: <a
                href="/samples/basic.yini"
                target="_blank"
                onclick="trackClick('basic.yiniClicked')">basic.yini</a
            >
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-2 py-4 text-center">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3">
            <a
                href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.getStarted.url}
                class="btn btn-primary"
            >
                Get Started
            </a>

            <a
                href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.examples.url}
                class="inline-block underline"
            >
                View Examples
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Quick Start -->
    <div class="try-under-a-minute full-bleed px-8">
        <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl pt-1 pb-6">
            <h2>⚡ Try YINI in under a minute</h2>
            <blockquote>
                This quick test uses the YINI CLI and requires Node.js and a
                terminal.
            </blockquote>
            <ul class="space-y-6">
                <li>
                    Save the two lines below as <code>config.yini</code>: <i
                    ></i><br />
                    <pre><code class="language-yini">{`^ App
name = "Hello"`}</code></pre>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        From your terminal (with Node.js installed), run this:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">npx yini-cli parse config.yini</code></pre>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-500">
                        JSON is the default output format.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>Results in JSON:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
    "App": {
        "name": "Hello"
    }
}`}</code></pre>
                </li>
            </ul>
            That's it.
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500">
                You just parsed your first YINI file.
            </p>
        </section>
    </div>

    <section class="my-6 pb-4">
        <h2>✨ What makes YINI different?</h2>
        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Clear structure without indentation rules</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">No fragile whitespace semantics.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Readable nesting that scales</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">
                    Files remain easy to scan even when large.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Predictable parsing</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">Defined by a formal grammar and spec.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Human-first design</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">
                    Comments, consistency, and low syntax noise.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    {/* Tooling + Practical usage section */}
    <section class="pt-4">
        <h2>💡 Use YINI in practice</h2>

        <p class="mt-1">
            Use YINI in real projects with practical tools for parsing,
            validation, and conversion.
        </p>

        <ul class="mt-2">
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                🛠 Validate and convert YINI files from the command line with
                the <a href="/tools/yini-cli">YINI CLI tool</a>.
                <div class="text-sm">
                    The CLI can output JSON, YAML, XML, and JavaScript.
                </div>
            </li>

            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                📦 Load YINI directly in TypeScript/JavaScript applications with
                the <a href="/tools/yini-parser-ts"
                    >YINI Parser for TypeScript</a
                >.
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                🐍 Load YINI directly in Python scripts, tools, and applications
                with the <a href="/tools/yini-parser-python"
                    >YINI Parser for Python</a
                >.
            </li>

            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                🧩 Use YINI for config files, tools, and <a
                    href="/use-yini/ci-with-yini-cli">CI workflows</a
                >.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="py-2">
        <h2>Lenient by default. Strict when needed.</h2>

        <p>
            YINI parsers are expected to use <strong
                >lenient mode by default</strong
            >. The <strong>strict mode</strong> is optional and intended for cases
            where stronger validation is needed.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Lenient mode</strong> — for everyday editing.
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Strict mode</strong> — for validation, robustness, and CI.
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Same format</strong> — different parser behavior.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            Lenient and strict mode are parser choices, not separate versions of
            the format.<br />
            The format is fully defined by the YINI specification.<br />
        </p>
        <p>
            This allows YINI to remain human-friendly by default, while still
            supporting stricter validation when needed.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="my-6 py-2 text-center">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3">
            <a
                href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.examples.url}
                class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg border px-4 py-2"
            >
                View Examples
            </a>

            <a
                href={CONFIG.urls.external.gitHub.yiniOrg}
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                onclick="trackClick('gitHub.yiniOrgCtaClicked')"
                class="inline-block underline"
            >
                View on GitHub ↗
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="my-6">
        <h2>⚖️ Quick comparison</h2>

        <h3>JSON vs YINI</h3>
        <p>JSON can become harder to read and maintain as files grow.</p>

        <h3>JSON:</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "Service": {
    "id": "aurora-gateway",
    "environment": "prod",
    "Limits": {
      "requestsPerMinute": 1200,
      "burst": 60,
      "maxBodyKb": 512
    },
    "Webhooks": {
      "enabled": true,
      "targets": [
        {
          "name": "alerts",
          "url": "https://hooks.example.com/alerts",
          "secret": "****"
        },
        {
          "name": "billing",
          "url": "https://hooks.example.com/billing",
          "secret": "****"
        }
      ]
    },
    "_comment": "JSON can't do comments; people add fields like this (or separate docs)."
  }
}`}</code></pre>

        <h3 class="pt-4">Here is the same config in YINI</h3>
        <p>
            YINI allows comments directly in the file and uses explicit section
            markers for structure.
        </p>

        <h3>YINI:</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-yini">{`/* Aurora Gateway service configuration (human-edited) */

^ Service
id          = "aurora-gateway"
environment = "prod"

^^ Limits
requestsPerMinute = 1200
burst             = 60
maxBodyKb         = 512

^^ Webhooks
enabled = true

targets = [
  { name: "alerts",  url: "https://hooks.example.com/alerts",  secret: "****" },
  { name: "billing", url: "https://hooks.example.com/billing", secret: "****" }
]`}</code></pre>

        <hr />

        <h3>YAML vs YINI</h3>
        <p>
            YAML can be sensitive to indentation and whitespace mistakes. YINI
            uses explicit section markers instead of indentation to define
            hierarchy.
        </p>

        <h3>YAML:</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-yaml">{`# One stray indent (whitespace) changes the structure

app:
  name: My App
  server:
    host: 127.0.0.1
    port: 8080
   allowedOrigins:   # <- off by one indent (hard to spot)
    - https://myapp.com
    - "http://localhost:3000"`}</code></pre>

        <h3>Here is the same config in YINI</h3>
        <h3>YINI:</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-yini">{`# Structure is defined by markers; indentation is optional

^ App
name = "My App"

^^ Server
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 8080
allowedOrigins = ["https://myapp.com", "http://localhost:3000"]
`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            <strong>Point:</strong> In YINI the hierarchy is signaled by markers,
            not whitespace.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="my-6">
        <h2>🧪 Real example</h2>
        <h3>YINI code:</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="/samples/settings.yini.png"
                ><img
                    class="mt-0 mb-1 rounded-lg border-2 border-black/90"
                    src="/samples/settings.yini.png"
                    alt="File settings.yini"
                /></a
            >
            Source file: <a
                href="/samples/settings.yini"
                target="_blank"
                onclick="trackClick('settings.yiniClicked')">settings.yini</a
            >
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500">
            This example uses features from the official YINI specification.
        </p>

        <h3>Parsed output (JSON):</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
    "Settings": {
        "serviceId": "NebulaService",
        "release": "3.2.1",
        "debugMode": false,
        "tagline": "\"Nebula\" is a cloud of gas and dust in outer space.",
        "Network": {
            "bindAddress": "127.0.0.1",
            "bindPort": 8080,
            "allowedOrigins": [
                "https://myapp.com",
                "http://localhost:3000"
            ]
        },
        "Capabilities": {
            "enableSearch": true,
            "experimental": [
                "new-ui",
                "streaming-api"
            ],
            "pools": {
                "min_units": 2,
                "max_units": 5,
                "size_mb": 128,
                "timeout_sec": 90
            }
        },
        "DB Config": {
            "host": "db.internal",
            "ssl": true,
            "Security": {
                "username": "service_user",
                "password": "****"
            }
        }
    }
}`}</code></pre>

        <p class="my-2 text-sm">
            New to YINI? Read a short <a
                href="/learn-yini/learn-yini-config-format"
                class="underline">learn the YINI format</a
            >.
        </p>
    </section>

    <hr />

    <section>
        <h2>🔭 Next steps</h2>
        <ul>
            <li class="text-lg">
                {
                    /*
                    STEP:    Get Started
                    PURPOSE: Immediate action
                    EFFECT:  Momentum
                    */
                }
                <a href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.getStarted.url}>Get Started</a
                ><br />
                <p class="mt-2">
                    Learn how to install and parse your first config in minutes.
                </p>
            </li>
            <!--li>
            <a href="/playground">Try it Online</a><br />
            <p class="mt-2">Try it in the Playground.</p>
        </li-->
            <li>
                {
                    /*
                    STEP:    Code Examples
                    PURPOSE: Visual reinforcement
                    EFFECT:  Trust
                    */
                }
                <a href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.examples.url}>Code Examples</a
                ><br />
                <p class="mt-2">
                    Real-world YINI examples showing common configuration
                    patterns.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                {
                    /*
                    STEP:    Specification
                    PURPOSE: Technical validation
                    EFFECT:  Commitment
                    */
                }
                <a href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.spec.url}>Specification</a><br />
